### Page 10 of 12

```text
Annexation – A-11-01, Overland Trails

Staff Report
May 6, 2011

Page 8

septic systems. Urban densities are most appropriately served by full-time professional
fire services and City police.
a. Including properties that are already served by some City services in the City’s
tax base allows for more equitable cost sharing among all City residents.
b. Full-time professional fire and police departments, which are available in the
City, increase the potential of quicker emergency responses, which is a benefit to
health, safety, and welfare.

2) The urban development of the area sought to be annexed would constitute a natural,
geographical, economical, and social part of the annexing city or town. Findings:  The
property is approximately 14.76% contiguous to the existing City boundary. The site is
located within the PlanCheyenne comprehensive plan area, which includes the
following land use designations on the future land use plan: Public and Quasi-Public for
the visitors center; Commercial Business where the three truck stops, hotel, and truck repair
shop are located, and Mixed Use-Employment where McDonalds is.
a. PlanCheyenne, which was adopted by the City of Cheyenne and Laramie County,
contemplates urban development in this area.
b. Urban Development at interchanges is a common occurrence in urban areas.  In
addition, this area is near major transportation hubs with both the intersections of
I-25 and I-80 and BNSF and UP Rail nearby. Urban development is expected near
such major transportation hubs. This area is approximately 2 miles from the
City’s original downtown. By comparison, the City has already grown
substantially further than two miles to the north and east. Balancing new
investment and growth in all directions is generally desirable both economically
and socially.
c. The City and Board of Public Utilities have plans to construct a water
transmission line approximately one half mile south of the proposed annexation.
This infrastructure will accommodate planned growth of the City to the south
beyond the proposed annexation area.

3) The area sought to be annexed is a logical and feasible addition to the annexing city or
town and the extension of basic and other services customarily available to the
residents of the city or town shall, within reason, be available to the area proposed to be
annexed. Findings:  The City has the capacity to serve more people and businesses
with its water and sewer system.  This area can be supported by City services, such as
fire protection, police protection, and garbage collection. The proximity to existing
urban development is an opportunity to provide City services in a fiscally responsible
manner.
a. Multiple connections to the City’s sanitary sewer system have already been made
within the annexation area and the properties have already agreed to be annexed
via outside user agreements.
b. The sewer line extending to these sites was oversized in anticipation of additional
development in this area to occur in the City. Annexation of these properties was
anticipated when the agreements to annex were executed.
```

